;; producer.sb — the OS-enforced capability boundary the usage-series producer
;; runs inside (issue #142; 2026-08-24 security review, round 3, finding 1).
;;
;; WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. The owner's ruling is that the step which walks raw
;; local transcripts must be STRUCTURALLY unable to start an agent session or
;; reach a network. Until this file, that claim rested on an AST lint over the
;; producer's import names. A lint over import NAMES cannot carry it, and the
;; review proved it with one line: `pathlib` is an admitted import and the
;; module object it binds re-exports `os`, so `pathlib.os.system(":")` restores
;; the launch callable while the import surface stays exactly the allowed set
;; and every producer test stays green. `sys.modules["os"]` is the same hole by
;; another spelling. Any module that itself imports `os` reopens it, so no
;; allowlist of import names can ever close it.
;;
;; A capability boundary has to be enforced by something that is not the
;; program's own source. On the workstation that runs this pipeline — the only
;; machine the producer ever runs on, under launchd — that something is the
;; kernel's sandbox, applied at the invocation layer by
;; scripts/usage-export/push-usage-series.sh before the interpreter starts.
;;
;; WHAT IT DENIES, and why exactly these two:
;;
;;   (deny process-fork)  No new process can be created, by any spelling.
;;       fork(2), vfork(2), posix_spawn(2) and everything layered on them —
;;       os.system, os.popen, subprocess, multiprocessing — fail with EPERM.
;;       This is the owner's "cannot spawn a session" ruling, enforced.
;;
;;   (deny network*)      No socket may be opened, bound, or connected, so no
;;       byte of a transcript can leave over a network from inside this step
;;       and no vendor API can be called. This is the "no path out" half.
;;
;; WHAT IT DELIBERATELY DOES NOT DENY, stated so the claim stays exact:
;;
;;   * exec(2) IN PLACE. sandbox-exec must be able to exec the interpreter
;;     itself, and seatbelt cannot express "allow exactly the first exec".
;;     Allowing exec by literal path is not portable either: the platform
;;     interpreter re-execs through a framework stub whose path moves with
;;     every toolchain and virtual environment, so a path allowlist here
;;     would be a fail-closed control that breaks the pipeline on a routine
;;     upgrade — worse than an honest bound. The residual is therefore that
;;     the producer could REPLACE ITSELF with another program. It cannot gain
;;     anything by doing so: the sandbox is inherited across exec, so whatever
;;     it became would still be unable to create a process or open a socket,
;;     and the export it was supposed to produce simply never appears, which
;;     the push script reports as a refusal.
;;   * the filesystem. `(allow default)` is deliberate. Denying by default
;;     means enumerating everything CPython touches to start — the dyld cache,
;;     the framework, the standard library, locale data, the temporary
;;     directory — which drifts with every interpreter release. The privacy
;;     boundary on what the producer READS and EMITS is a different control,
;;     enforced in the producer itself (the closed emission vocabulary and
;;     `assert_only_dates_and_integers`) and by the rooted, no-follow, bounded
;;     walk. This profile bounds CAPABILITY, not paths.
;;
;; Pinned by scripts/ci/test_usage_export_scripts.py: the profile's text is
;; asserted line for line, the push script is proven to invoke the producer
;; through it, and on a Darwin host the suite additionally EXECUTES the
;; boundary and requires a spawn attempt and a connect attempt to fail.
(version 1)
(allow default)
(deny process-fork)
(deny network*)
